A host runs a folder's own extensions' commands through the exec a user
extension sets, and refuses them until one does.

A host runs every agent command in a sandbox, but pi.exec ran outside it for
every extension. So a folder's own extension, such as one that lints each
file the agent writes, could run a program whose settings any sandboxed
command can write, and those settings could run code outside the sandbox.

pi.setProjectExec lets a user extension, once loaded, set how a project
extension's exec runs; the harness sets one that runs it in the session's
sandbox. A project extension's exec goes through it, given the folder it
would run in, and is refused while none is set. pi names an extension's
scope only after every extension has loaded, so while loading, one under the
folder's own .pi, where pi installs a folder's packages, counts as the
folder's. No extension may set it while loading, and only a user extension
may set it at all. User and command-line extensions run commands as before.

Apply only to the host's private pi build; ordinary interactive pi stays
unchanged.

